<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neville family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Neville, Nevilles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nevilles were a powerful English noble family from the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, earls of Westmorland and Salisbury. Through his marriage to the Beauchamp heiress, Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick, known as the Kingmaker, gained the lordship of Glamorgan and became the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> until he was killed at Barnet in 1471.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neville-family.jpg" alt="Neville family" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Immanuelle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_-_Neville,_Earls_of_Westmorland,_and_Barons_Neville_of_Raby.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the male line the family was of native English origin and may have belonged to the pre-Conquest aristocracy of Northumbria. Dolfin, son of Uhtred, is first recorded in 1129 holding the manor of Staindrop in County Durham from the Prior of Durham, and Raby, in that parish, remained the principal seat until 1569. The name came through a marriage: Isabel de Neville, heiress of the Anglo-Norman Geoffrey de Neville, married Robert FitzMaldred, and their son Geoffrey (died c. 1242) took his mother's more prestigious surname while keeping his father's arms, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> saltire on red.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family rose through royal office in the north, as sheriffs, castellans and justices. Ralph Neville was summoned to the House of Lords at its establishment in 1295, and from 1334 the Nevilles were regularly wardens of the marches against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In 1346 a later Ralph Neville commanded the army that defeated the Scots at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Battle of Neville's Cross</a> and captured King David II. In 1397 Richard II created Ralph Neville Earl of Westmorland, and by then only the Percy Earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> matched the family's power in the north, which bred a bitter rivalry between the two houses.</p><p>Earl Ralph's second marriage, to Joan Beaufort, half-sister of Henry IV, split the family: the sons of this marriage received most of the lands, and the Nevilles of Middleham fell out with the Nevilles of Raby, who kept the earldom. Joan's son Richard Neville became Earl of Salisbury by marriage, and his son Richard became Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a> the same way. Salisbury and Warwick were the chief supporters of Richard, Duke of York, who had married Salisbury's sister Cecily, and after Edward IV took the throne in 1461 John Neville received the earldom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> in 1465. Warwick later broke with Edward over the king's marriage to Elizabeth Woodville and rebelled in 1469 and 1470.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the deaths of Warwick and his brother John, Marquess of Montagu, in 1471, their lands were divided between the king's brothers George Plantagenet, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarence</a>, and Richard, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, who made Middleham Castle his main residence. The Raby line, Lancastrian throughout the wars, survived much diminished. In 1569 Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, joined the Percys in the Revolt of the Northern Earls against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>; he fled abroad, was attainted and died in 1601 without a male heir. The Bergavenny branch endured, later as Earls and then Marquesses of Abergavenny, at Eridge Park in East Sussex, held by the family since 1448.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Neville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
